Z||000000||frontal lobe||08-01-2000||12:25 AM||||Well, the '87 GT has got to go.  I originally got an '86GT with 120,000 to be the donor for a v8 conversion.  I got it in Oct.  Then in January I found a great LT1 from a Buick Roadmaster with only 30,000 miles on it.  I got down to Archie's in March for his open house.  Then in April I saw it:  a maroon '87 GT 5-speed with only 63,000 miles on it!  It's history was it was kept in Florida by someone who went back and forth from Wisconsin to Florida (snowbirds, we call them).  I was going to use this for the conversion and have an EXCELLENT, MINT v8 Fiero.  However, after driving it, it is just too awesome as is to mess with it.  So then I was going to keep it and go back to the '86GT for the conversion.  Well then I could tell (she didn't say anything or nag, she's great, but we've been married 12 years--you just know) my wife didn't think it was fair that I have two "play" cars and she had none.  So I just bought her a '95 Miata, which she loves.  That makes 5 cars with a 2 car garage.  So it's either build a new garage (too expensive) or sell a car.  So the '87GT has to go!!!!  I'm glad you can't see me now--I start to choke up every time I say this.  But then I go out and look at the '86GT in the driveway, and know things will be O.K.<P>At the March Archie open house I found out that if I use the LT1 I will have to move the engine over, notch the frame, and get different axles.  So I think I'll sell the LT1 and look for a different 350.  Does anyone know if I can get a carbureted 350 through Wisconsin emissions????||||reg||1
